<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#lingua-franca">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingua franca</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lingua franca is a language used for communication between speakers of different native languages. Akkadian served this role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> in the trading world of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. English became a global lingua franca largely through the reach of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p></div>
